;;; A derived activator: a rule derives an activator that binds a relation
;;; parameter to a fact, and the loader refuses the program for it. The
;;; witness of regression `derived-activator/derived-activator`; its twin, where no run
;;; derives the activator, is `derived_activator_never.ein`.
;;;
;;; `r`'s parameter `?R` names a relation, `(box (?R b))`, and `mk` derives
;;; `r`'s activator from `item`'s argument, which a declared relation's
;;; argument is: any value, a fact among them. `(item (a b))` makes `mk`
;;; derive `(r (a b))`, which names a relation by a fact, and a fact names
;;; none. The loader refuses the program, on every command, with one line
;;; naming `mk`, its conclusion and the premise that can bind `?f` to a fact
;;; (Q-M1f.1, Q-M1f.41).
(relation box Node :why "{?3} is in the box")
(relation hit Node :why "{?3} is hit")
(relation item Node :why "{?3} is an item")
(box (a b))
(rule r (?R)
  :match (box (?R b))
  :assert (hit z)
  :why "a box holds a {?R} of b")
(rule mk ()
  :match (item ?f)
  :assert (r ?f)
  :why "{?f} is an item, so r is asked of it")
(item (a b))
